---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Restaurant",
  name: "The Keep Kitchen & Liquor Bar",
  servesCuisine: ["French", "American", "Contemporary"],
  priceRange: "$$$",
  telephone: "+1-614-224-9500",
  url: "http://www.thekeepcolumbus.com/",
  address: {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    streetAddress: "50 W Broad St",
    addressLocality: "Columbus",
    addressRegion: "OH",
    postalCode: "43215",
    addressCountry: "US",
  },
  description:
    "Modern French brasserie and speakeasy-style liquor bar inside the historic LeVeque Tower in downtown Columbus, Ohio.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Keep Kitchen Menu Guide (2026) — Prices & Picks"
  description="An independent guide to The Keep Kitchen & Liquor Bar at Hotel LeVeque in Columbus: French brasserie classics, the speakeasy bar, reported prices, and visiting tips. Prices reported as of October 2026."
  path="/keep-menu/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogImage="/images/the-keep-dining.webp"
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
    <img
      src="/images/the-keep-dining.webp"
      alt="Illustration of The Keep's 1920s art-deco French brasserie in Columbus"
      class="w-full h-64 sm:h-96 object-cover rounded-2xl mt-8"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />

    <p class="mt-8 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-gold-dark">
      Menu Guide · Downtown Columbus, Ohio
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mt-2 leading-tight">
      The Keep Kitchen &amp; Liquor Bar at Hotel LeVeque — Menu Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-soft">By <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Firoz Khan</span> <span class="text-gold-dark">&middot;</span> FK Digital Media</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      The Keep Kitchen &amp; Liquor Bar sits on the mezzanine level of the historic LeVeque
      Tower at 50 West Broad Street in downtown Columbus — an art-deco landmark completed in
      1927, and the setting for one of Ohio's most atmospheric hotel dining rooms. The
      concept is a modern French brasserie with a speakeasy-style liquor bar: gas lamps,
      brass, and leather, with classic dishes reinvented using local Ohio ingredients. Next
      door to the Palace Theatre, it's the natural pre-show dinner. Below is our independent
      walkthrough of the menu, reported prices, and what we'd order. Everything is written in
      our own words; prices are reported figures, not a copy of the restaurant's menu.
    </p>

    <!-- At a glance -->
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="!mt-0 font-display text-xl font-bold">At a glance</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-sm">
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Location</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">50 W Broad St (mezzanine level, LeVeque Tower), Columbus, OH 43215 — Hotel LeVeque</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Concept</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Modern French brasserie + speakeasy liquor bar</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Cuisine</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">French classics with Midwestern ingredients</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Price level</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Upscale; happy hour 4–6pm daily is the value play</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Reservations</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Recommended, especially pre-theatre nights</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Phone</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">+1 (614) 224-9500</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-guide">
      <h2>The menu: French classics, Ohio ingredients</h2>
      <p>
        The menu runs the French canon with local sourcing: brie fondue, beef carpaccio,
        steak frites, beef bourguignon, shrimp linguini, French onion soup, moules, and a
        bouillabaisse. A 2018 published review priced the moules around <strong>$16</strong>,
        steak tartare at <strong>$15</strong>, French onion soup at <strong>$8</strong>, the
        bouillabaisse at <strong>$28</strong>, and steak frites with bordelaise at
        <strong>$24</strong> — dated figures, but they show the menu's mid-range-to-upscale
        shape. Recent diners rave about the butterscotch crème brûlée, the brie fondue, and a
        hot-honey crispy chicken sandwich at lunch.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our take: the French onion soup is the sleeper hit — reviewers call it a contender for
        the best in the city — and the steak frites is the safe, satisfying center of the menu.
      </p>

      <h2>The liquor bar</h2>
      <p>
        The "liquor bar" half of the name is earned: old-school cocktails get a modern remake
        with house-made syrups and botanicals, and the barrel-aged Manhattan is the order to
        know. There's a daily happy hour Monday–Friday from 4pm to 6pm with specially priced
        drinks and shareable snacks — the smartest way into the room. The bar runs late on
        weekends (till 1am Friday and Saturday).
      </p>

      <h2>Hours</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Monday–Thursday:</strong> breakfast 7am–10:30am, lunch 11am–1:30pm, dinner 5pm–9pm</li>
        <li><strong>Friday:</strong> dinner to 10pm</li>
        <li><strong>Saturday:</strong> brunch 7am–1:30pm, dinner 5pm–10pm</li>
        <li><strong>Sunday:</strong> brunch 7am–1:30pm, dinner 5pm–9pm</li>
        <li><strong>Bar:</strong> Sunday–Thursday 4pm–11pm; Friday–Saturday 3pm–1am</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we'd order</h2>
      <p>If we had one meal here, this is how we'd play it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>To start:</strong> the brie fondue for the table — it's the dish people photograph.</li>
        <li><strong>The main event:</strong> steak frites with bordelaise, the brasserie staple done right.</li>
        <li><strong>At the bar:</strong> a barrel-aged Manhattan at happy hour, timed before a Palace Theatre show.</li>
        <li><strong>To finish:</strong> the butterscotch crème brûlée — the dessert reviewers won't stop talking about.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Planning your visit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pre-theatre timing:</strong> book dinner before a Palace Theatre show — the room is built for it, and the bar does the post-show nightcap.</li>
        <li><strong>Private dining:</strong> the Coleman Room hosts private events for up to 40 guests.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget:</strong> upscale but not punishing — plan on a mid-range downtown dinner, less if you lean on happy hour.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Price disclaimer -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-gold/40 bg-gold/10 p-5 text-sm text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink mb-1">About these prices</p>
      <p>
        Individual dish prices above come from a <strong>published 2018 review</strong> and are
        likely dated — we present them as the menu's shape, not current figures. Always confirm
        prices with the restaurant before visiting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-ink-soft">
      <a href="/best-hotel-restaurants-ohio/" class="text-gold-dark font-semibold hover:underline">← Back to Ohio hotel restaurants</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
